TIMEQUAKE by Kurt Vonnegut

1998. Vintage. Paperback.

Kurt Vonnegut is an author I've read a lot of in my twenties and maybe
this is the right age to read him; in your university years and for a
few years afterwards; these should also be your peak Art-Cinemagoing
years. Once you're older with a wife and a mortgage and family
commitments, your reading tastes should mature somewhat.

I read about three-quarters of Vonnegut's works back in the day; the
essential books are Player Piano, Slaughterhouse Five and Breakfast of
Champions. Some of his short stories like Welcome to the Monkey House!
and Harrison Bergeron are classics in their own right. Another
favourite of mine, title forgotten, invovled a man forced to play
piano in a seedy jazz club to make ends meet for his family. It turns
out that the man is quite well off and has arranged his financial
affairs quite strangely so that he'll need the jazz club money and he
just really likes playing piano in jazz clubs.

One of my favourte parts of Palm Sunday is Vonnegut's happy/sad x/y
graphs of tales like Cinderella, where someone loses it all and
regains most of it and has learned valuable lessons along the way. But
then there's tales like Kafka's Metamorphosis, where an already
misearable man turns into an insect; this is illustrated by a start
position well below the x axis that immediately dumps down to negative
infinity.

In Timequake, Vonnegut passes amusing opinions on Ernest
Hemingway. The back cover copy astutely mentions that suicides are an
important part of this book.

The 10-year Timequake device isn't that interesting and only serves to
create a few cataclysmic accidents when the 10 year rewind finishes
and everyone is so used to having no free will that there are plane
crashes and traffic accidents galore.

Kurt seems to be trying a fusion of writing styles here; in short
bursts like his book Hocus Pocus. There's Vonnegut autobiography,
Kilgore Trout sci-fi fiction makebelieve and a standard fictional
narrative. Vonnegut mentions this is what is left of an earlier
attempt, Timequake 1, that he'd worked on for years and been forced to
abandon and use whatever parts were salvageable in this book. It seems
Vonnegut is trying to give us an insight into the writing process.

After the 9/11 attacks of 2001, Kurt Vonnegut came out and said
America deserved it, and it's been hard to respect him once I found
this out. Timequake was enjoyable enough, and it keep me entertained
during an all-weekend event in Darling Harbour's Tumbalong Park, but I
would have been happier still if I respected Vonnegut more
politically.

